00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.230
from our time here at Building Radar

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.140
but also before at consulting

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.160
on how to interact with clients
and conducting workshops.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.060
And third, I would also
talk about some general tips

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.590
about being a manager
in a startup company,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.880
interacting like that.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.010
So we just finished working
on the document for tomorrow,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.410
it's already quite late,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.297
so we are about to go to bed, but Leopold,

00:00:57.297 --> 00:00:59.530
thanks for joining me here.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.760
I do not only know Leopold from work,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.870
but we also met already during university

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.090
when we studied at our
undergraduate degree.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.110
So I know that you had many
other opportunities as well.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.740
You would also have had the opportunity

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.130
to go into consulting, into banking,

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.294
but you decided to start a business,

00:01:15.294 --> 00:01:17.920
start a startup after university.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.080
I trust many of you
also would be interested

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.510
to hear what was your rationale for that?

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.640
How did you think about that at that time?

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.110
- Yeah, first of all, Heinrich,
thank you for having me.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:33.110
And really for me, it
all came down to impact.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.200
Starting a startup is
really all about impact

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.370
during my time at
Google, I really noticed,

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:44.600
hey, this is really great
here, it's a nice tech company.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.660
it's a fast changing environment,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.240
but I just couldn't figure out for myself

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.960
what specific impact
I had for the company.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.420
And I realized, really the only way

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.560
to have this impact you want
to have as an entrepreneur

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.290
is if you start your own company.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.040
That's why I decided to
start Building Radar.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.950
And our ambition really to
generate something big here.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.250
- So we shot lots of material
at our workshop at Koster,

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.830
and we created a little
clip, a little mini video

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.810
to give an impression
how these workshops work,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.310
what we do there and
how all this plays down.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.720
So I will show you this video now

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.870
to give an impression of how
we do it at Building Radar.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.450
Our mission at Building
Radar is to help companies

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.220
in the construction industry

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.370
to increase their revenue
with revenue engineering.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.800
Many construction companies we talk to,

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.930
tell us that all of their functions

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.640
have very detailed sales processes

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.900
with the exception of the sales function.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.340
This is of course surprising

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.310
because in the end of the
day, sales pays for the party.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.140
Revenue engineering is the idea

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.920
that the sales process
can be broken down in

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.020
small steps, like a manufacturing process.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.140
And then each individual
step can be trained

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.280
and can be improved in a systematic way.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.110
When we work together
with new client of ours,

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:09.540
the starting point is
usually a workshop week.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.440
In the workshop week, we
want to do three things.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.640
First, we want to size
the revenue potential,

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.130
offer revenue engineering of this new

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.440
and innovative way to conduct proactive

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.580
sales for the organization.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.510
To do that, we conduct interviews

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.920
in this workshop week
with different employees

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.770
of the company across hierarchy levels.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.270
We also conduct a conversation

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.990
with the managerial accounting team

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:35.120
to collect all the necessary data

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.750
and also conduct interviews
with the IT department,

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.713
to understand all the dependencies.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.140
As a result of that, we can come up

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.270
with a very clear picture
what the revenue potential

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.850
for that individual company
with the revenue engineering is,

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.970
over the next two years.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.620
Second, we look at the organization

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.700
and capabilities of that client.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.060
So from all the interviews,

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.060
conversations and other
data sources that we have,

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.550
we assess the current organization

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.020
and understand to what extent

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.580
it is already fit for revenue engineering,

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.660
or what the potential gaps are,

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.220
that still need to be
closed by the management,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.650
by the team, in order to be successful

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.530
with revenue engineering
over the next two years.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.160
Third, we jointly develop a roadmap,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.640
how a successful path towards revenue

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.630
engineering can look like.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.800
Here we distinguish all the individual

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.960
building blocks that this organization

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.360
needs to have in place

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.180
and still needs to come up
with in order to be successful.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.930
We develop a timeline
how that could look like,

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.920
and we also show a different path

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.610
with different pathways out there,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.117
how the folk would actually get there.

00:04:40.117 --> 00:04:41.590
At the end of this workshop week,

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.280
we have a full day with
the senior leadership

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.860
of the company where we share
our preliminary results,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.040
jointly co-create some
of the missing parts

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.830
that still need to be developed

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.840
and then jointly agree
on how the next steps

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.430
in the journey for revenue
engineering should look like.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.800
Today, we conducted the last workshop day,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.250
with the senior leadership of Koster,

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.300
which is one of the leading
general contractors in Germany.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.190
We had a very productive day and exchange

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.790
and are very much looking
forward to the next steps.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.780
Workshops are a key part
of working in consulting,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.660
also many other
professional services firms.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.810
And the key reason is
that usually you do not

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.250
just want to sit alone in the team room,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.230
work on something for several months,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.970
and then at the end of the project,

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:32.300
send over the results to the clients

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.520
because this will really not
help to really create buy-in.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:36.870
So instead, what you want to do

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.130
is you want to co-create
results with your client

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.900
and surely workshops are
a great way to do that.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.360
So let me share three
tips from our time here

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.720
at Building Radar but also
before in-consulting at McKinsey,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.150
on what you can do to
improve your workshop game.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.920
And the first is really to draw from many,

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.370
many different data sources,
information sources,

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.920
that then you bring to the workshop.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.020
And what very few people only do,

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.590
but what is often extremely powerful

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.280
is to talk to other
employees of the company.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.170
Because if you do that, what you achieve

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.200
is that then when you talk to the leaders,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.080
the decision makers,
you can also tell them

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.520
what their employees told you.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.100
And this clearly adds to your credibility

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.790
because the truth is that in many,

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.120
especially of these larger organizations,

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.410
the senior executives, the leaders,

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:30.200
they do not really know what
on an operational level,

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.270
all the people are doing.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.700
They often have a certain
preconception of that,

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.650
but it's not always accurate

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.230
what they think of what is happening.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.640
My second tip is about
creating the perception

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.470
with the client, that the
workshop was a success

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:44.430
and that some very, very tangible

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.150
results are coming out of that.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.850
Because as a matter of truth,

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.840
if you just sit there, present something,

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.930
maybe ask a couple of questions,

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:54.240
ideally people will
have learned something,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.730
but what most people will like even more

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:00.300
if the workshop really
created some tangible results

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.700
that they can then later use

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:03.310
and then build on and work with,

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.330
and what we like to do both in consulting

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.250
but also Building Radar,
is to in the workshop,

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:11.240
jointly develop things on the flip chart,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.130
come up with decisions,
come up with results.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:16.430
And then what we like to
do is after the workshop,

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:19.780
we create a document with
all the things we worked on,

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:20.940
all the things we can work with,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.040
and then we distribute this document

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.820
with all the workshop participants.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.910
And this makes it very tangible,

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.650
very clear what you
have jointly developed,

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.120
what the results were,

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.400
and this then often creates
the perception in your clients

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.760
that yes, the workshop
was not a waste of time,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.850
but highly productive and
something came out of that,

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:42.700
which surely was worth
the time and the money

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.360
of all the people that attended.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.170
My third tip is about how to make

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.570
the workshop a memorable experience.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.640
And here, my key insight
that I learned over time

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.610
is that the more different types of media,

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.450
the more different types of formats

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.120
that you use in the workshop,

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:01.110
usually the better and the
better will be the memory

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.580
that people have about it.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.220
And what I mean by that is the following:

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.840
So if the whole workshop consists

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.220
of you standing in front of the
crowd, presenting something,

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.900
asking questions, this is not
necessarily a bad workshop,

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.240
but what's often even better,

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.340
if then you have parts of the workshop,

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.450
which are conducted in
front of the flip chart.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:19.490
Now you're standing there,

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:21.870
you're jointly developing something.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.410
Maybe you even have another part

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.810
where you have a whiteboard or a poster,

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.100
where you elaborate something

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:29.630
that then comes together
written on the poster,

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.080
maybe you have some sticky notes

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.190
that people can write on
attached to the poster,

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:35.590
of course you should not do this,

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.270
only for the sake of doing this,

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:39.270
this should always also just add value

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.590
and make sense in the overall context.

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:43.617
But I would encourage you
to really think about that.

00:08:43.617 --> 00:08:45.390
And just to add something on top

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.890
of just you standing
in front of the people

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:49.400
and presenting something.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.000
So now I'm standing here outside

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.780
of our office here at Building Radar.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:54.610
Hope today's video was insightful

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.630
to get some impressions at how we work

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:57.780
and what it is like,

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.710
and some tips on how to conduct strong

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:01.420
and successful workshops.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:04.270
